WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.410
Charles Dickens is one of the best-known writers
in the world, and is considered to be the

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:06.890
greatest novelist of the Victorian Era.

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:10.740
If you live in an English-speaking country,
you probably read Great Expectations, Oliver

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.360
Twist, and A Christmas Carol in school.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:18.050
His work became so popular, it even sparked
the creation of the world “Dickensian”

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:19.500
to describe his style.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:25.010
So, why is Charles Dickens’ work considered
to be so good that it has inspired future

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:26.010
generations?

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:34.720
And who was the man who created all of these
well-known characters?

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:46.030
Early Life

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:51.050
Charles Dickens was born on February 7, 1812,
in Portsmouth, England to John and Elizabeth

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:52.050
Dickens.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:55.940
He was just one of 8 children, and two of
his siblings died at a very young age.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:00.760
Throughout his childhood, young Charles loved
to play outside, and he read as many books

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:02.120
as he possibly could.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:06.210
His father, John Dickens was a clerk in the
Navy Pay Office, which meant that he guarded

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:09.240
money in a safe and handed out paychecks to
sailors.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:12.440
The Navy would often move him from place to
place whenever it was necessary.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:16.540
So as a boy, Charles was able to live in several
different areas of England.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:20.430
John Dickens was a very ambitious man, and
hoped to climb the ranks at his job.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:24.470
Eventually, he was able to move the family
to London and pay for Charles to get a private

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:25.470
education.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:29.159
Once he was living in the big city, Charles
was exposed to performances from the local

00:01:29.159 --> 00:01:30.159
theater.

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:34.780
He became inspired by actors, and would jump
up on the tables of the local pub to sing

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:35.780
and dance.

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:40.640
He also performed plays with his siblings,
and imagined growing up to be an actor.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.050
His father John was optimistic, and did not
prescribe to the old-fashioned notion that

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:49.700
people are born in the place they were supposed
to stay throughout their lives.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:53.060
He taught all of his children that they could
be rich if they worked hard enough.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:57.380
One day, while walking with Charles, he pointed
to a mansion called Gads Hill Place, saying

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:01.200
that if he worked hard, he could own a house
like that someday.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.409
Even though John Dickens was a hard worker
and an excellent father, he made the mistake

00:02:05.409 --> 00:02:08.509
of spending his money before he could make
it.

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:12.750
With so many children and a wife to take care
of, he lived beyond his means.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:16.620
His father struggled so much with money, that
Charles Dickens had to leave school to work

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.690
in a blacking polish factory at 12 years old.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:24.760
He had the tedious task of gluing labels to
bottles for 11 hours a day, making just 6

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:25.940
shillings per week.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:30.920
Even though this was clearly child labor,
it was totally legal at the time.

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:34.959
Charles was actually lucky to have found that
job, though, because his father ended up in

00:02:34.959 --> 00:02:35.959
a debtor’s prison.

00:02:35.959 --> 00:02:40.569
John owed 40 pounds and 3 shillings, and the
only way he could get out was to pay back

00:02:40.569 --> 00:02:41.569
his debt.

00:02:41.569 --> 00:02:46.270
But obviously, if he was not allowed to work
or earn money in prison, he could not be released.

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:50.540
The entire Dickens family had to move into
the prison with him- even the young children-

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:53.290
because there was nowhere else for them to
live.

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:56.370
Since twelve year old Charles was the only
one who actually had a job, he could afford

00:02:56.370 --> 00:03:00.770
to rent a room that was just big enough for
himself, and the responsibility of freeing

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:04.580
his entire family fell on his small shoulders.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:08.370
Charles visited his family every Sunday, and
helped them in any way he could.

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:11.989
However, since he only made 6 shillings a
week, there was not much the poor boy could

00:03:11.989 --> 00:03:13.050
do.

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:17.940
John Dickens was able to inherit some money
from his mother, and pay off his debt in full.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:21.580
Even though he only had to endure this lifestyle
for a year, and he was eventually able to

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:25.950
return to school, this deeply affected Charles
Dickens for the rest of his life.

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:30.040
They had been fortunate enough to receive
the inheritance to save the family just in

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:33.220
time, but what about the people who were not
so lucky?

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:38.220
Charles realized that even well-to-do-families
full of good people like his could fall on

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:39.220
hard times.

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:43.480
Years later, this would inspire his stories
Oliver Twist and Little Dorrit.

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:47.100
In 1827, Charles became a junior law clerk
for an attorney at 15 years old, and he began

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:48.580
to learn how to write shorthand.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:52.090
While he worked at the firm, he would pay
attention to the clients who were coming in

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:53.599
looking for legal advice.

00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:57.950
He often wrote down their physical descriptions
and mannerisms, and wrote stories about them.

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:02.180
Charles had a knack for finding humor in even
the most awful situations, and would often

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:04.959
laugh at some of the issues that people were
complaining about.

00:04:04.959 --> 00:04:09.110
This was just the beginning of his fascination
with true crime, as well as his ability to

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:12.700
write about difficult topics with a sense
of humor.

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:16.699
Now that he was growing up and had more experience
in the workforce, Charles Dickens tried to

00:04:16.699 --> 00:04:18.749
go after his dream of becoming an actor.

00:04:18.749 --> 00:04:22.189
When he was 20 years old, he had an audition
for a play Covent Garden.

00:04:22.189 --> 00:04:26.020
But he came down with a terrible cold, and
had to miss his appointment.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:27.400
He was never given another chance.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:32.169
At the time, it must have seemed like a horrible
setback, because it had been his life-long

00:04:32.169 --> 00:04:33.169
dream.

00:04:33.169 --> 00:04:37.470
But if he had become an actor, he would have
never fulfilled his destiny of being a great

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:38.470
writer.

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:42.449
He eventually moved on from being a law clerk
and became a journalist at the Morning Chronicle.

00:04:42.449 --> 00:04:46.249
Even at the paper, he was still interested
in law, and sat through court cases at the

00:04:46.249 --> 00:04:48.789
House of Commons to report what had happened.

00:04:48.789 --> 00:04:53.300
This is where he met the daughter of his editor,
Catherine Hogarth.

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:56.849
After only knowing one another for three months,
Charles proposed, and they were married in

00:04:56.849 --> 00:04:57.870
1836.

00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:03.029
They would go on to have ten children throughout
the course of their marriage.

00:05:03.029 --> 00:05:05.749
Writing Career

00:05:05.749 --> 00:05:11.629
Through his writing, Charles Dickens paints
the Victorian Era as a time that was dirty,

00:05:11.629 --> 00:05:14.639
dark, and crude, especially for those who
were living in the lower class.

00:05:14.639 --> 00:05:19.210
While some of his social commentary is very
critical, it’s clear that he truly did love

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:21.409
England, and he wanted it to be better.

00:05:21.409 --> 00:05:25.680
He felt that the best way he could help was
to write stories that put a spotlight on social

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:26.949
and political issues.

00:05:26.949 --> 00:05:30.580
It wasn’t all dark, of course, Dickens also
wrote touching scenes where people could enjoy

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:35.680
the simple things in life, like a warm fire,
a good story, and spending time with family.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:39.710
In fact, his story called A Christmas Carol
is credited for capturing all of England’s

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:43.889
best holiday traditions, and it still warms
people’s hearts to this very day.

00:05:43.889 --> 00:05:48.619
In all of his stories, there was always an
underlying moral lesson, and one of the first

00:05:48.619 --> 00:05:51.500
issues he decided to tackle was the treatment
of the poor.

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:55.589
The beginning of the Victorian Era was at
the height of the Industrial Revolution, and

00:05:55.589 --> 00:06:00.389
it was now more necessary than ever to get
cheap labor into factories of the country

00:06:00.389 --> 00:06:03.659
wanted to move forward in economic progress.

00:06:03.659 --> 00:06:07.939
When Charles was 22 years old, The 1834 Poor
Law made it a rule that if anyone was living

00:06:07.939 --> 00:06:11.190
on the street, they were taken to live in
a workhouse, and forced to work in a factory.

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:14.569
Children were educated while the parents would
work several hours a day in exchange for food

00:06:14.569 --> 00:06:15.569
and shelter.

00:06:15.569 --> 00:06:20.360
This caused a huge amount of debate, because
workhouses were essentially prisons for poor

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:21.360
people.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:24.009
Once they entered a workhouse, there was no
escape.

00:06:24.009 --> 00:06:28.080
Everyone knew that life in the workhouse was
awful, but people in the upper class were

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:31.060
taught that poverty was someone’s own fault.

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:35.050
Any harsh treatment was necessary, because
they deserved it.

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:39.360
As someone who grew up suffering through poverty,
Charles Dickens knew that good, hard-working

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:43.509
people could sometimes fall on hard times,
and that doesn’t mean they should be thrown

00:06:43.509 --> 00:06:44.509
in a workhouse.

00:06:44.509 --> 00:06:49.000
It was clear to him that the lawmakers from
the upper class were out of touch with reality,

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:52.059
and totally lacked empathy for people in the
working class.

00:06:52.059 --> 00:06:56.619
It became his mission to change the hearts
and minds of the people of England.

00:06:56.619 --> 00:07:00.779
He knew two very important things that set
him apart from other writers: Firstly, no

00:07:00.779 --> 00:07:02.710
one likes being lectured to.

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:06.479
Second is that his audience would never care
about anything unless it had something to

00:07:06.479 --> 00:07:08.129
do with them.

00:07:08.129 --> 00:07:12.909
So Dickens had to figure out how to make a
story entertaining first, and almost hide

00:07:12.909 --> 00:07:15.669
his moral lessons within the context of the
story.

00:07:15.669 --> 00:07:20.509
He would also incorporate people in the middle-to-upper
classes, and show that even having a privileged

00:07:20.509 --> 00:07:24.349
birth does not necessarily mean you are immune
to hardship.

00:07:24.349 --> 00:07:28.819
When he was 24 years old, he began publishing
his stories in serialized form.

00:07:28.819 --> 00:07:33.319
These were The Pickwick Papers, which was
a satire based on real-life political corruption.

00:07:33.319 --> 00:07:38.289
At first, the stories were not so successful,
and Dickens was only earning 14 pounds per

00:07:38.289 --> 00:07:39.289
month.

00:07:39.289 --> 00:07:42.990
But after the fourth issue was published,
it became a huge hit, and sales of his serial

00:07:42.990 --> 00:07:46.860
went from 400 to 40,000 copies per month.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:51.110
The fact that his work was serialized a few
chapters at a time rather than releasing a

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:53.999
full novel all at once was actually a huge
benefit to him.

00:07:53.999 --> 00:07:58.089
His fans would speculate what was going to
happen next, just like someone watching a

00:07:58.089 --> 00:08:00.460
TV series in modern times.

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:05.209
People were so excited to talk about the stories
to their friends, that it soon spread by word

00:08:05.209 --> 00:08:06.209
of mouth.

00:08:06.209 --> 00:08:10.449
Soon, the name Charles Dickens was being mentioned
all over London, and he became a celebrity

00:08:10.449 --> 00:08:11.449
writer.

00:08:11.449 --> 00:08:15.259
At age 25, Dickens would publish his serialized
story called Oliver Twist, which showed what

00:08:15.259 --> 00:08:18.270
life was truly like for a child living in
the workhouse.

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:22.539
The main character, Oliver, was born into
a well-to-do-family, and he had the misfortune

00:08:22.539 --> 00:08:25.180
of getting lost and ending up in the workhouse.

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:27.900
Readers were able to think, “What if this
had happened to me?”

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:31.270
He was sure to make the stories humorous and
easy for anyone to read.

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:35.640
More and more people began to have empathy
for the poor, and understand that something

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:37.140
needed to be changed.

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:41.349
This was years before the internet, of course,
so the only way Charles Dickens could do his

00:08:41.349 --> 00:08:46.190
research about the biggest social issues throughout
England was to act like a reporter, and travel

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:47.850
to locations in person.

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:52.400
When he heard that young boys were being abused
in orphanages, he took a two-day carriage

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:56.500
journey to visit one so that he could have
a first-hand account of what was truly going

00:08:56.500 --> 00:08:57.500
on.

00:08:57.500 --> 00:08:59.180
This inspired his story called Nicholas Nickleby.

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:04.260
He also visited insane asylums to witness
the mistreatment of people with mental illnesses,

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:06.950
and often walked through some of the worst
parts of London.

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:11.300
So you might be wondering- Charles Dickens
showed up to witness people being abused,

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:14.310
and he didn’t do anything about it except
write a book?

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:19.560
He never claimed to have all of the solutions
to the problems in society, and with his experience

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:23.801
sitting as a reporter in court, he knew that
changing a law was a complicated ordeal.

00:09:23.801 --> 00:09:28.210
All he could do was simply observe, and write
stories about the truth.

00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:32.380
This alone was enough for people to talk about
the issues, and begin to have empathy for

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:33.660
the down-trodden.

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:35.770
Everyone wanted their country to be better.

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:40.290
In that way, there has perhaps been no other
man alive who has done so much for social

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:43.660
reform in the United Kingdom that Charles
Dickens.

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:49.810
On the Road, and Making A Difference

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:53.050
Even though Charles Dickens was never able
to fulfill his childhood dream of becoming

00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:56.200
a professional actor, he decided to try becoming
a showman.

00:09:56.200 --> 00:10:01.720
In 1842, Charles and his wife Catherine decided
to travel to the United States together so

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:03.560
that he could give public readings of his
work.

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:07.940
At the time, books were one of the main forms
of entertainment, so the idea of a favorite

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:11.420
author coming to town was like a rock star
going on tour.

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:15.790
Dickens insisted that tickets should be discounted
for working-class people, and he spoke to

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:17.970
sold out-theaters every time.

00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:22.130
People who witnessed his readings described
how passionately he would read in the voices

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:26.510
of each of his characters, and that no other
performance of his works would ever rival

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:27.510
the author.

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:32.950
In 1844 and 45, he spent time living in Italy
and Switzerland doing public speaking engagements,

00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:34.410
and wrote a few more books.

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:38.470
By 1847, he had earned such a substantial
amount of money from his tour that he was

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:42.410
finally able to invest some of the money into
actually taking action on helping people.

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:46.510
At the time, there were so few job opportunities
and child care options for women.

00:10:46.510 --> 00:10:49.850
Their only option was to get married and find
a man to take care of them.

00:10:49.850 --> 00:10:54.210
And if they fell on hard times, they had no
choice but to become “fallen women”, or

00:10:54.210 --> 00:10:55.210
sex workers.

00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:59.200
If they were arrested, many of these women
were sent to “Magdalen Hospitals”- (Yep,

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:00.630
like Mary Magdalen from the Bible.)

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:04.220
They were constantly told they were an awful
person, and they were punished harshly for

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:05.220
their sins.

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:09.280
The only other option for an uneducated woman
besides the life of a sex worker, would be

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:12.460
to live in the work house, which was essentially
going to prison.

00:11:12.460 --> 00:11:15.480
At least, with sex work, they got to be free.

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:19.530
Charles Dickens knew that many of these women
did not deserve to be in this situation.

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:23.780
And many would be able to turn their lives
around, if only they were given a place to

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:25.860
stay to get their life together.

00:11:25.860 --> 00:11:29.070
He founded the Home for Homeless Women at
Urania Cottage.

00:11:29.070 --> 00:11:31.720
The women who lived here were taught skills
that would get them jobs.

00:11:31.720 --> 00:11:35.810
And, of course, he would also interview them
to hear their life stories, and he was able

00:11:35.810 --> 00:11:40.020
to draw inspiration from them for his future
works.

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:44.870
A New Chapter

00:11:44.870 --> 00:11:49.360
In March of 1856, Dickens was 44 years old
when he learned that the mansion he had dreamed

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:52.160
of as a child, Gads Hill Place, was up for
sale.

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:55.490
He purchased the home for £1,790.

00:11:55.490 --> 00:11:59.031
At the time, he had let go of his childish
wonder of the place, and thought that at the

00:11:59.031 --> 00:12:02.600
very least, it might make a good investment
to rent it out.

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:06.080
But once he saw it in person again, he truly
loved the home, and it reminded him of what

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:08.870
had inspired him to work towards so long ago.

00:12:08.870 --> 00:12:12.410
He kept it as his country house, and it became
a welcome escape from the city.

00:12:12.410 --> 00:12:16.080
It was around this time that Charles Dickens
was beginning to have a bit of a crisis.

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:20.220
He was at the peak of his career, but no longer
happy in his marriage with Catherine, and

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:23.340
spent more time throwing large parties at
Gad’s Hill Place.

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:26.850
At the time, divorce was totally taboo, especially
for a public figure.

00:12:26.850 --> 00:12:31.140
He had also given himself this reputation
of being a pinnacle of morality, and had to

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:33.810
continue pretending that everything was fine.

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:38.120
At one of these parties, Dickens invited over
the cast of the local theater, as well as

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:41.090
celebrity friends like author Hans Christian
Anderson.

00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:45.530
It was around this time in his life when Dickens
met a young actress named Ellen Ternan.

00:12:45.530 --> 00:12:47.920
He was 45, and she was just 18 years old.

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:52.200
They began having an affair, but he kept their
relationship a secret, as not to damage their

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:53.200
reputations.

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:56.240
In 1858, Charles became separated from his
wife Catherine.

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:59.610
They no longer lived together, but he was
forced to stay legally married.

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:03.120
During their separation, Charles tried to
get a divorce by claiming that his wife was

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:04.120
mentally ill.

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:06.330
The word soon got out, and it caused a public
scandal.

00:13:06.330 --> 00:13:11.280
He wrote 98 letters about his issues with
his wife, and they were only recently discovered

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:13.830
when they were sold at auction in 2014.

00:13:13.830 --> 00:13:16.080
This has caused a lot of debate among scholars.

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:19.590
Some say that Dickens was lying about his
wife’s mental state, and willing to have

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:23.920
her committed in an asylum just so he could
be with Ellen Ternan.

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:27.370
Others believe that Catherine truly was sick,
and that this man who had so much love and

00:13:27.370 --> 00:13:31.260
empathy for other people that he would never
send his wife to an asylum if it were not

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:32.260
necessary.

00:13:32.260 --> 00:13:35.440
Charles Dickens continued his relationship
with Ellen behind closed doors, and bought

00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:38.630
her a house just outside of London so that
he could go to visit her.

00:13:38.630 --> 00:13:41.360
The two of them continued their secret affair
for 13 years.

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:45.550
They had a child together, but he died in
infancy, and they would never go on to have

00:13:45.550 --> 00:13:46.550
another.

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:47.570
On June 9th, 1865.

00:13:47.570 --> 00:13:54.260
Charles and Ellen were on a train together
when they approached Staplehurst.

00:13:54.260 --> 00:13:57.720
The tracks were being repaired, and the conductor
had no idea that it was happening.

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:00.490
The conductor hit the breaks, but it was already
too late.

00:14:00.490 --> 00:14:03.850
All of the first class carriages fell into
the river below.

00:14:03.850 --> 00:14:08.590
The one and only first class car that remained
on the tracks held Charles Dickens, and it

00:14:08.590 --> 00:14:09.970
dangled over the edge.

00:14:09.970 --> 00:14:13.160
The second class carriages behind him were
enough weight to hold on.

00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:15.800
Charles jumped into the water to try to help
and of the survivors.

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:20.440
If he found someone who was just barely clinging
on to life, he would hold them, and tell them

00:14:20.440 --> 00:14:21.680
to take a swig of brandy.

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:23.590
More than one person died in his arms.

00:14:23.590 --> 00:14:27.560
A total of 10 were killed in the accident,
and many others were seriously injured.

00:14:27.560 --> 00:14:30.840
Just as he was helping bring the bodies out
of the water, he suddenly remembered that

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:34.990
the manuscript for his latest book, Our Mutual
Friend, was still inside of the train car.

00:14:34.990 --> 00:14:38.670
He risked his life to go inside and retrieve
it before it went over the edge.

00:14:38.670 --> 00:14:42.779
For the rest of his life, Charles Dickens
avoided traveling, and getting on a train

00:14:42.779 --> 00:14:44.529
gave him anxiety.

00:14:44.529 --> 00:14:48.570
For years, no one knew about Charles Dickens’
secret relationship, because Ellen Ternan

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:50.170
had burned most of their letters.

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:53.430
Eventually, historians began to piece together
the evidence throughout the years.

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:59.220
A movie about Ellen Ternan’s life and relationship
to Dickens was made in 2012 called The Invisible

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:00.610
Woman.

00:15:00.610 --> 00:15:04.770
Death, and Legacy

00:15:04.770 --> 00:15:08.420
After the accident, Dickens health was suffering.

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:12.280
In 1867, he was given the opportunity to go
on another speaking tour of America, they

00:15:12.280 --> 00:15:15.160
offered to pay him 20,000 pounds after expenses.

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:17.360
This is nearly a million pounds in today’s
money.

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:22.090
He was still suffering from a lot of travel
anxiety, and potentially had PTSD, but Dickens

00:15:22.090 --> 00:15:24.630
felt that he could not say “no” to that
kind of money.

00:15:24.630 --> 00:15:29.060
But when he returned, it was clear to everyone
that his health had seriously declined.

00:15:29.060 --> 00:15:34.240
In 1870, Charles Dickens had a stroke, and
died unexpectedly in his home at Gads Hill

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:35.240
Place.

00:15:35.240 --> 00:15:38.779
He was 58 years old, and had believed that
he would have lived a much longer life.

00:15:38.779 --> 00:15:42.589
Dickens worked up until the day he died, and
the last novel that he had been working on

00:15:42.589 --> 00:15:45.620
was called Edwin Drood, which involved a murder
mystery.

00:15:45.620 --> 00:15:50.110
He died before revealing the end, which caused
his fans to debate for years over what they

00:15:50.110 --> 00:15:51.780
true ending must have been.

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:55.870
When he died, his estate was worth 93,000
pounds, which is close to 4 million today.

00:15:55.870 --> 00:16:00.270
In his will, he stated that he wanted a very
quiet and private funeral in Rochester.

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:03.920
However, this sparked a debate, because many
people wanted to pay their respects.

00:16:03.920 --> 00:16:07.770
So his family decided to allow him to be buried
in Westminster Abbey.

00:16:07.770 --> 00:16:11.370
In the days following his death, crowds of
people showed up to pay respect to his open

00:16:11.370 --> 00:16:12.370
casket.

00:16:12.370 --> 00:16:15.540
There were so many flowers laid on top of
him, that no one could see him anymore.

00:16:15.540 --> 00:16:19.610
Even though he is gone, the legacy that Charles
Dickens left behind will last forever.

00:16:19.610 --> 00:16:23.230
Today, his work is still read and analyzed
in schools all over the world.

00:16:23.230 --> 00:16:27.860
Oliver Twist was made into a musical called
Oliver, and there are dozens of film adaptations

00:16:27.860 --> 00:16:28.990
of his stories.

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:33.900
Every December, families all over the globe
re-visit the stories of Bob Cratchet and Ebinezer

00:16:33.900 --> 00:16:35.730
Scrooge in A Christmas Carol.

00:16:35.730 --> 00:16:40.060
There is almost no way to properly end a video
on a man who has accomplished so much, so

00:16:40.060 --> 00:16:42.230
what better way to do it than his own words?

00:16:42.230 --> 00:16:46.779
He once said, “Whatever I have tried to
do in life, I have tried with all my heart

00:16:46.779 --> 00:16:51.980
to do it well; whatever I have devoted myself
to, I have devoted myself completely; in great

00:16:51.980 --> 00:17:01.740
aims and in small I have always thoroughly
been

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:14.180
in earnest.”

